    "final_answer": "I found a relevant report on BBC News about recent developments in renewable energy technologies in the UK. Here is the summary:\n\n## Article Found on BBC News\n\n**Title:** \"Plans for £16m renewable energy research centre in Ayrshire scrapped\"\n\n**Date:** 10 September 2026\n\n**Source:** BBC News (Section: Glasgow & West Scotland / Scotland)\n\n**URL:** https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cdxkk3v2gk7o\n\n### Key Details:\n\n- **The Story:** Plans to build a £16m renewable energy research centre in Ayrshire have been abandoned. The proposed **Community Renewable Energy (CoRE) Research and Innovation Centre** was to be located at Knockroon, near Cumnock, and was intended to become a regional hub for renewable energy innovation in the UK.\n\n- **Original Plans:** The centre of excellence was to include research and development facilities and two Passivhaus demonstrator homes (energy-efficient homes that maintain an almost constant temperature).\n\n- **Revised Plans:** East Ayrshire Council has overhauled the project. The new direction aims to:\n  - Establish three vocational training hubs in Cumnock and Dalmellington\n  - Redirect money toward other renewable energy projects in local communities\n\n- **Funding:** CoRE is a £24.5m project, comprising **£17m from the UK government** and **£7.5m from East Ayrshire Council**.\n\n- **Goals:** The project aims to use the transition to renewable energy to create jobs, improve communities, and tackle fuel poverty.\n\n- **Reasons for Change:** Officials cited the pandemic and cost-of-living crisis as factors. The original rationale for a standalone centre cited a \"lack of locally skilled workers,\" and discussions with businesses, education providers, and employers consistently identified gaps that prompted the overhaul.\n\nThis is a substantive BBC News report on recent UK renewable energy developments, particularly focusing on the shift in Scotland's approach to renewable energy research infrastructure and skills development.",
